{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Upon a נס.</b> Upon a pole, which they call פירק\"א בְּלַעַ\"ז — and likewise ״וְכַנֵּס עַל הַגִּבְעָה״ (Isaiah 30), ״אָרִים נִסִּי״ (ibid. 49), ״שְׂאוּ נֵס״ (ibid. 13); and because it is high, for a sign and for viewing, he calls it נס." "<b>Anyone who is bitten.</b> Even if a dog or a donkey bit him he would be injured and deteriorate and weaken — but a snakebite hastens to kill; therefore here it says וְרָאָה אֹתוֹ — mere looking — whereas for snakebite it says ״וְהִבִּיט״ — ״וְהָיָה אִם נָשַׁךְ הַנָּחָשׁ אֶת אִישׁ וְהִבִּיט וגו׳״ — because one bitten by a snake would not recover quickly unless he looked at it with intention; and our Sages said: And does a snake kill or give life? Rather, whenever Israel would look upward and subordinate their hearts to their Father in heaven they would be healed, and if not, they would perish (Rosh HaShanah 29)."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "the la'az is Old French perche, 'pole'"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:11:54.531644261Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}